Idiopathic Neurogenic Chronic Cough (Laryngeal Sensory Neuropathy)
Geriatric & Frailty
Chronic persistent dry cough, Throat tickle/laryngeal paresthesia, Triggered by non-tussive stimuli, Refractory to standard therapies

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-026
Reason for Visit: Acute severe paroxysmal coughing resulting in a minor syncopal episode and profound exhaustion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient suffering from relentless, violent coughing fits leading to an acute episode of fainting (cough syncope) and an inability to breathe or speak comfortably. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out an acute cardiopulmonary emergency (e.g., severe asthma exacerbation, pulmonary embolism, or foreign body aspiration) prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I cannot stop this tickle in my throat; I cough until I gag, and I actually passed out earlier from the effort."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic, persistent dry cough lasting longer than 8 weeks, which originated shortly after a mild upper respiratory tract viral infection. The cough is characterized by a relentless sensation of a tickle, scratch, or localized irritant in the throat (laryngeal paresthesia). The patient notes the cough is violently triggered by non-tussive stimuli such as simply talking, laughing, or exposure to cold air (allotussia). Tonight, these triggers led to severe paroxysmal coughing episodes resulting in gagging, retching, and a brief syncopal episode (cough syncope) witnessed by the spouse. The patient states this condition is entirely refractory to standard therapies, having failed trials of inhaled corticosteroids, proton pump inhibitors, and nasal sprays over the past two months. The patient frequently experiences mild dysphonia (hoarseness) following the coughing fits. They describe a disproportionate negative impact on their quality of life, avoiding social interactions entirely due to the unpredictable coughing.
Functional Status: Usually independent; currently exhausted and fearful of triggering another fit by speaking.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent syncope leading to traumatic injury (falls).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Appears exhausted, clutching a glass of water, speaking very slowly to avoid triggering the cough.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam is completely unremarkable: clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheeze, no stridor, and normal air entry. Cardiovascular exam reveals normal heart sounds without murmurs. Oropharyngeal examination shows mild posterior pharyngeal erythema consistent with chronic mechanical irritation from coughing, but no acute tonsillitis or postnasal drip. Neck exam reveals no lymphadenopathy or thyroid masses. Neurological exam reveals no focal deficits post-syncope. (Exclusion of pulmonary aetiologies via normal chest radiograph and PFTs is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding, confirming the sensory neuropathy diagnosis).
Environmental Observations: The room temperature is cool, which the patient identifies as an active trigger.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is currently taking a dry-powder inhaler (fluticasone), which may ironically be exacerbating the laryngeal irritation. No ACE inhibitors are prescribed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Idiopathic Neurogenic Chronic Cough (Laryngeal Sensory Neuropathy) with acute cough syncope - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Pertussis (Whooping Cough) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound chronicity of the symptoms (>8 weeks) without the classic acute catarrhal phase, and the clear absence of an inspiratory 'whoop' or post-tussive emesis, though testing may be considered in hours.

Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of wheeze, chest tightness, or shortness of breath between coughing fits, and the documented failure of prior inhaled corticosteroid trials.

Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of heartburn, water brash, or worsening of symptoms purely when supine, alongside the failure of prior high-dose PPI therapy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Risk of trauma from recurrent cough syncope)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe manifestation of an established, refractory chronic cough, specifically a cough syncope episode. Telephone triage appropriately identified the need to rule out acute airway compromise or a primary cardiac syncopal event. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in acute distress, risking further syncopal falls and extreme physical exhaustion. This urgent after-hours intervention successfully ruled out acute lower respiratory tract pathology (asthma, pneumonia), established the likely neuropathic nature of the upper airway hyperreactivity, and allowed for the initiation of targeted neuromodulator therapy to dampen the heightened cough reflex, safely averting an emergency department presentation for a benign but highly distressing symptom.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommended immediate cessation of the dry-powder steroid inhaler, as the powder itself can act as a laryngeal irritant. Commenced a low-dose trial of a neuromodulator (e.g., amitriptyline 10mg at night or gabapentin 100mg at night, pending local guidelines) to dampen the laryngeal sensory nerve hyper-responsiveness.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured "swallow and sip" replacement behavior. Step 1: At the very first sensation of the throat tickle, before the cough begins, the patient executes a hard, deliberate swallow followed immediately by a small sip of room-temperature water. Step 2: Upon successfully suppressing the urge to cough using this method, the spouse provides immediate verbal praise and the patient notes the success in a small bedside tally book. Cause-and-effect: Replacing the reflex to violently clear the throat with a competing motor action (swallowing) directly interrupts the neuropathic cough cycle, while the tally provides tangible proof of control, reducing the severe anxiety associated with the condition.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a speech pathologist for specialized laryngeal desensitization techniques.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgently recommended the patient sit down immediately at the first sign of a severe coughing fit to prevent traumatic falls from recurrent syncope.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 1-2 weeks to titrate the neuromodulator dosage and review the speech pathology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient experiences continuous coughing leading to blue lips (cyanosis), severe chest pain, coughing up frank blood (haemoptysis), or a prolonged loss of consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical exam shows your lungs are clear; this cough is likely a nerve issue in the throat, which requires specific nerve-calming medications rather than standard cough syrups.
Clinical Course: The new medication will take several days to build up and calm the nerves. You will likely feel drowsy initially. The coughing fits will not stop immediately but should steadily reduce in severity and frequency.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you start coughing up blood, if your lips turn blue, or if you experience severe chest pain. You must see your GP next week to check if the new nerve medication needs adjusting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A previous virus has damaged the tiny sensory nerves in your throat, making them hyper-sensitive. They are incorrectly sending "tickle" signals to your brain when you just breathe cold air or talk. The new medication works by calming these specific nerves down, stopping the false signals.
Actionable Steps: Stop using the dry powder inhaler. Take the nerve medication exactly as prescribed before bed. When you feel the tickle, force a hard swallow and sip water instead of coughing, and mark your successes in your tally book.

